udy on enteral tolerance post-injury, also published in the JPEN Journal, adds valuable insights into the management of patients recovering from injuries. This research is essential for developing standardized protocols that enhance patient care in clinical settings. The journal's role in disseminating such research highlights its impact on clinical practices and patient outcomes. [Data: Entities (12934), Relationships (19296)]\n\n## Focus on nutrition support strategies\n\nThe JPEN Journal frequently discusses and publishes nutrition support strategies, making it a key player in the field of clinical nutrition. The journal's emphasis on evidence-based practices helps healthcare providers stay informed about the latest advancements and guidelines, which can lead to improved patient care and outcomes. This focus on nutrition support is critical, especially in settings where patients require specialized feeding protocols. [Data: Relationships (16048)]\n\n## Guidelines on total parenteral nutrition\n\nThe JPEN Journal publishes research and guidelines on the management of patients receiving total parenteral nutrition, which is crucial for patients who cannot receive nutrition through traditional means. This aspect of the journal's work is vital for ensuring that healthcare providers have access to the latest evidence and best practices, ultimately impacting patient safety and recovery. [Data: Relationships (16140)]", "# Enhanced Recovery After Surgery Community\n\nThe community focuses on Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) protocols, which aim to improve recovery outcomes for surgical patients through multimodal rehabilitation and nutritional support. Key entities include Norway, Kehlet, and various surgical procedures, all interconnected through their contributions to ERAS and the importance of hospitalization in patient recovery.\n\n## Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) as a pivotal program\n\nERAS is a significant initiative designed to enhance recovery for patients undergoing elective surgery. It incorporates multimodal rehabilitation strategies that focus on various aspects of patient care, including nutrition, perioperative care, and fast-track surgery protocols. The program aims to optimize recovery times and reduce hospital stays, which can lead to lower healthcare costs and improved patient outcomes. The relationships between ERAS and various surgical procedures highlight its comprehensive approach to patient recovery. [Data: Entities (12583); Relationships (11390, 19196, 19198)]\n\n## Norway's contributions to international health standards\n\nNorway plays a crucial role in global health initiatives, particularly through its contributions to the World Health Organization's growth reference patterns for children. This involvement underscores Norway's commitment to advancing health standards and practices internationally. Additionally, Norway is one of the founding countries of the ERAS group, which emphasizes its influence in shaping surgical recovery protocols. The interconnectedness of Norway with ERAS highlights the importance of international collaboration in healthcare improvements. [Data: Entities (12586); Relationships (5965, 19191)]\n\n## Kehlet's foundational role in ERAS\n\nKehlet is recognized for proposing innovative strategies for perioperative management that led to the establishment of the ERAS program. His work focuses on reducing surgical stress and enhancing recovery, which are central tenets of ERAS. The relationship between Kehlet and ERAS illustrates the importance of research and clinical practice in developing effective recovery protocols. This foundational role is critical for understanding the evolution of surgical recovery practices. [Data: Entities (12584); Relationships (19192)]\n\n## The significance of nutritional support in hospitalization\n\nHospitalization is a critical context for addressing patient nutritional needs, especially for older adults and those with conditions like sar